const august13ReplacementSeeds: readonly August13Seed[] = [
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-earnings-code-governance',title:'Philippines payroll earnings-code governance',excerpt:'Research on keeping earnings codes interpretable when payroll inputs, approvals, and reporting classifications change.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Bureau of Internal Revenue publishes official tax resources that employers should interpret with qualified advisers.',focus:'Earnings-code governance is a classification problem: a code must explain what it represents, when it applies, and who can approve its use.',evidence:'For a sampled code, record its business meaning, eligible population, pay-period effect, reporting treatment, effective date, owner, and approval source. Compare the code used in the payroll result with the approved definition rather than relying on a familiar label.',measure:'Measure the share of sampled earnings lines with a current definition, effective date, source, reviewer, and reporting destination. Segment results by regular earnings, allowances, deductions, and one-time items across three pay periods.',risk:'A code can calculate correctly while reporting incorrectly. Ambiguous labels, copied legacy codes, and undocumented exceptions make later reconciliation difficult and can cause two teams to interpret the same input differently.',limitation:'Tax and reporting treatment depends on employer facts and current Philippine rules. This framework does not classify a particular payment or replace advice from the relevant authority and qualified tax professional.',conclusion:'Treat each earnings code as governed reference data. A small, dated definition and approval trail gives payroll reviewers a way to challenge unclear classifications before they become recurring errors.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-preparation-reconciliation',title:'Philippines payroll preparation reconciliation',excerpt:'A bounded study of reconciling prepared payroll inputs to the approved population before final review.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Philippine Statistics Authority publishes labor-force statistics with defined populations and reference periods.',focus:'Preparation reconciliation asks whether the records prepared for a pay period are the records that were authorized for that period.',evidence:'Define the employee population, pay period, source extract, inclusion and exclusion rules, and preparation timestamp. Compare counts and key totals, then list additions, removals, and changed records with a reason and accountable reviewer.',measure:'Report population match rate, unexplained additions, unexplained omissions, and changed-field counts per pay period. A three-cycle sample can distinguish one-off corrections from a recurring intake or cutoff problem.',risk:'A matching total can hide a wrong population. An omitted employee and an extra employee may offset financially, so count and identity checks must accompany amount comparisons.',limitation:'Reconciliation thresholds are control choices, not legal conclusions. Payroll systems and employer arrangements differ, and qualified owners must determine whether an exception can be approved.',conclusion:'Reconcile the population before interpreting the total. The strongest evidence connects the approved roster, prepared result, differences, explanations, and final decision in one reviewable chain.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-effective-date-conflicts',title:'Philippines payroll effective-date conflicts',excerpt:'Research on finding and resolving conflicting effective dates in employee and payroll changes.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Department of Labor and Employment publishes Philippine labor resources and advisories for employers and workers.',focus:'Effective-date conflict is a temporal data problem: a valid change can still produce the wrong payroll result when its applicable period is unclear.',evidence:'For each sampled change, retain the request date, source date, effective date, pay period, cutoff status, reviewer, and resulting payroll treatment. When sources disagree, preserve both observations and identify the authorized decision rather than overwriting the conflict.',measure:'Count conflicts per 100 changes and segment them by field, source, pay period, and discovery point. Track how many were found before preparation, during review, or after the payroll result was issued.',risk:'Late discovery compresses the correction window and encourages informal fixes. A date copied from a message or file name can look precise while lacking an authoritative basis.',limitation:'The correct effective date depends on the employment event, contract, policy, jurisdiction, and current rules. This article does not decide entitlement or retroactive treatment.',conclusion:'Make dates first-class evidence. A reviewer should be able to see which source governs, which period is affected, and who accepted the consequence of a conflict.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-employee-population-reconciliation',title:'Philippines payroll employee-population reconciliation',excerpt:'An evidence-led method for reconciling active employee populations across payroll, human-resources, and approval records.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The National Privacy Commission describes accountability obligations for personal-information processing under Republic Act No. 10173.',focus:'Population reconciliation protects payroll review by making inclusion, exclusion, and status assumptions visible before amounts are assessed.',evidence:'Choose a reference period and compare the authorized employee population with the payroll input population. Record identifier, status, start or end event, source system, effective date, reviewer, and explanation for every difference.',measure:'Calculate matched, missing, unexpected, and unresolved records as a percentage of the defined population. Repeat the comparison for at least three pay periods and keep status changes separate from data-quality defects.',risk:'An apparently small count difference can be material when it concerns a leaver, joiner, or sensitive status change. Totals alone do not show who was included or excluded.',limitation:'Privacy safeguards and employment obligations vary by context. Use minimum necessary fields and obtain qualified interpretation for retention, status, and payroll treatment decisions.',conclusion:'Start with the population, not the total. A dated exception list lets the authorized payroll owner decide whether each difference is expected, corrected, or escalated.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-manual-adjustment-trend',title:'Philippines payroll manual-adjustment trend',excerpt:'How to study recurring manual payroll adjustments without treating every adjustment as an error.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Philippine Development Plan 2023–2028 uses indicators and monitoring to connect activity with accountable outcomes.',focus:'Manual-adjustment analysis separates legitimate judgment from recurring upstream defects that deserve a different control.',evidence:'For every sampled adjustment, record the pay period, field or amount, reason category, source evidence, preparer, approver, effective date, and whether the adjustment reversed or recurred. Preserve the original result and the approved change.',measure:'Report adjustments per 100 payroll records, by category and pay period. Pair frequency with value, affected population, lead time, and repeat rate so a small but sensitive adjustment is not hidden by volume.',risk:'A lower adjustment count can mean fewer problems or less recording. A high count can reflect stronger detection. Without reason codes and source review, the metric invites the wrong conclusion.',limitation:'Adjustment acceptability depends on employer policy, contracts, system behavior, and current legal or tax requirements. The framework does not approve a specific correction.',conclusion:'Use trends to target investigation, not to ban judgment. The useful question is whether each adjustment is explainable, authorized, and becoming less repetitive for a defined reason.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-approval-evidence-chain',title:'Philippines payroll approval evidence chain',excerpt:'A research framework for linking payroll preparation, review questions, approval authority, and the final decision.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The National Privacy Commission publishes guidance for organizations handling personal information and security safeguards.',focus:'Approval evidence is a chain of reasoning, not merely a name or timestamp attached to a payroll file.',evidence:'Connect the prepared population, source records, reviewer questions, responses, decision authority, final approval, and resulting file version. Record what was reviewed, what remained unresolved, and the period to which the decision applies.',measure:'Sample approvals across three pay periods and score whether the evidence identifies scope, reviewer, authority, decision, timestamp, exceptions, and final artifact. Report missing elements separately rather than using one opaque completeness score.',risk:'A valid user account can approve the wrong file. A copied sign-off can also conceal unanswered questions, especially when deadlines make the approval look routine.',limitation:'Approval segregation and authority levels depend on the organization and applicable rules. This research does not define who may approve payroll or release funds.',conclusion:'Make the approved object unmistakable. A later reviewer should be able to distinguish preparation, challenge, authorization, and release evidence without reconstructing an informal conversation.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-late-input-cause-analysis',title:'Philippines payroll late-input cause analysis',excerpt:'A bounded method for identifying why payroll inputs arrive late and whether the delay threatens safe review.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Philippine Statistics Authority publishes employment measures using explicit reference periods and population definitions.',focus:'Late-input analysis is useful only when it distinguishes source availability, handoff delay, requester delay, and review capacity.',evidence:'Timestamp the expected arrival, actual arrival, cutoff, first review, clarification request, and resolution. Classify the cause using the evidence available at the time, and record whether the item affected the pay-period decision.',measure:'Report late inputs per 100 expected inputs, median and 90th-percentile lateness, cause category, and unresolved count at cutoff. Compare ordinary, holiday, and handoff periods instead of averaging them together.',risk:'A team may improve the late rate by moving the expected deadline or excluding difficult items. Stable definitions and an audit of sampled timestamps are needed to keep the metric honest.',limitation:'A delay metric does not establish fault or legal consequence. Local calendars, contracts, service scope, and authorized decisions determine what action is appropriate.',conclusion:'Use lateness as an early warning signal. The practical control is an owned exception with enough time for qualified review, not an automatic acceptance of incomplete input.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-correction-reason-coding',title:'Philippines payroll correction reason coding',excerpt:'Research on classifying payroll corrections so recurring causes can be measured without exposing unnecessary employee information.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Data Privacy Act establishes obligations for organizations processing personal information in the Philippines.',focus:'Correction coding turns isolated rework into evidence about source quality, timing, interpretation, and system behavior.',evidence:'Use a minimum necessary record containing pay period, affected field or category, correction reason, source, discovery point, approver, effective date, and final outcome. Avoid copying unrelated personal data into the analysis set.',measure:'Count corrections per 100 records by reason, discovery stage, and recurrence. Review a fixed sample each month to test whether the assigned reason matches the underlying source and decision evidence.',risk:'A vague label such as “other” prevents learning, while an over-detailed label can expose sensitive information. Coding can also become punitive if it is treated as a person score rather than a process signal.',limitation:'Reason categories should fit the organization’s systems and privacy policy. They do not determine tax treatment, employee entitlement, or individual responsibility.',conclusion:'Design categories for action. A useful code tells the owner whether to improve intake, timing, interpretation, system configuration, or review, while preserving only the data needed to support that conclusion.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-pay-period-definition',title:'Philippines payroll pay-period definition',excerpt:'How precise period definitions improve payroll comparisons, exception handling, and source reconciliation.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The Philippine Development Plan 2023–2028 emphasizes measurable indicators and implementation monitoring.',focus:'A pay-period definition is the boundary that makes a payroll comparison meaningful; without it, records from adjacent periods can appear to conflict.',evidence:'Record the period start and end, cutoff, processing date, payment date where relevant, timezone, business-day convention, and source version. Use the same period key across payroll inputs, approvals, reports, and reconciliation evidence.',measure:'Sample records around period boundaries and count misclassified events, late entries, duplicate inclusion, and unresolved period questions. Analyze at least three cycles with ordinary and exceptional inputs.',risk:'A transaction can be valid but assigned to the wrong period. Comparing totals without period and population keys can create false variances or conceal a real omission.',limitation:'Period design must follow the employer’s contract, system configuration, calendar, and applicable rules. This article does not determine pay-date or filing obligations.',conclusion:'Treat the period key as shared evidence. A precise boundary reduces avoidable debate and gives reviewers a common basis for deciding how late or corrected events should be handled.'},
  {slug:'philippines-payroll-review-sample-design',title:'Philippines payroll review sample design',excerpt:'A practical research model for choosing representative payroll review samples while preserving attention to sensitive exceptions.',published:'2026-08-13',stat:'The International Labour Organization provides labor and employment research and country resources for the Philippines.',focus:'Sample design determines what a payroll review can reasonably say about a cycle; convenience samples cannot support broad conclusions.',evidence:'Define the population, period, unit, inclusion rules, sample size, selection method, and exception oversample before reviewing records. Separate ordinary records from high-risk changes, corrections, and late inputs, then document each finding against its source.',measure:'Report sample coverage, exception discovery rate, evidence completeness, and unresolved findings by stratum. Repeat the design across three pay periods and retain the reason for any change in method.',risk:'Reviewing only easy records produces false confidence, while reviewing only exceptions says nothing about ordinary processing. A large sample with unclear selection is also difficult to interpret.',limitation:'No sample proves that every payroll record is correct. Appropriate confidence, coverage, and review effort depend on population size, risk, and the organization’s control objectives.',conclusion:'Define what the sample can establish before collecting it. Combine representative coverage with targeted review of sensitive exceptions, and let qualified owners interpret findings before changing the process.'}
] as const;

const august13Body = (s: August13Seed) => [
  `${s.focus} ${s.evidence} ${s.stat}`,
  `The unit of analysis should be one payroll event or one pay-period decision, not an entire shared folder. Define the population, period, and responsible owner before collecting records. ${s.measure}`,
  `A good study separates preparation from judgment. A support specialist may gather approved inputs, compare fields, and describe an exception, while the authorized payroll owner decides whether a sensitive change, filing, release, or correction should proceed. ${s.risk}`,
  `Source quality matters as much as arithmetic. Use an authoritative record for the event, preserve the relevant version, and record the date on which it became effective. When two systems disagree, retain both observations and describe the reconciliation decision instead of silently overwriting one value.`,
  `Timing should be explicit. State the pay period, cutoff, review window, and timezone where relevant. A record received after cutoff is not automatically wrong, but its late status should be visible and should route to the person who can decide the consequence.`,
  `Evidence should be proportionate. Keep the fields needed to understand the decision, link to the approved source, and avoid copying an entire employee file into a working tracker. Access should follow the task, with named accounts and a review when the assignment or role changes.`,
  `Metrics help management see a pattern, but they need definitions. State the denominator, unit, period, and exclusion rules. A rate per 100 records, a median age in hours, or a count of unresolved items at cutoff can be compared only when the population remains stable.`,
  `The control should have a stop rule. Pause when the source is missing, the request is inconsistent, the reviewer lacks authority, or the effective date cannot be established. Escalation is evidence of a functioning control, not proof that the process failed.`,
  `A pilot across three pay periods can test whether the record is practical. Review one ordinary cycle, one cycle with late or unusual inputs, and one cycle with a handoff or absence. Compare evidence completeness and unresolved work, then revise the record fields before broadening the lane.`,
  `Interpret findings in context. A higher exception count may reflect better detection rather than worse payroll quality, while a lower count may mean the team stopped recording problems. Pair quantitative results with a review of sampled source records and owner decisions.`,
  `The principal limitation is scope. ${s.limitation} This framework supports disciplined inquiry, but it is not tax, employment, privacy, banking, or legal advice. Rules and system behavior should be checked against current authoritative guidance.`,
  `For an outsourced payroll lane, the client retains decision accountability even when preparation is performed elsewhere. The handoff should identify what was checked, what remains open, who owns the next decision, and when that decision is due.`,
  `The conclusion is bounded rather than absolute. ${s.conclusion} The practical test is whether another authorized reviewer can reconstruct the result from the retained evidence without relying on an undocumented conversation.`,
  `A mature review also asks what should not be retained or automated. Remove duplicate exports, limit sensitive fields, and do not let a threshold replace human approval. Use the evidence to make the next cycle easier to inspect, not merely faster to close.`
].join(' ');

